<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Menteshe</h1><p class="st-meta">1261 CE – 1424 CE (approx.) · capital Milas</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkish beylik on the Carian coast of south-western Anatolia, known for its naval raids into the Aegean.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the 1260s and 1270s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> raiders led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menteshe-bey">Menteshe Bey</a> occupied the Byzantine coastlands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hecatomnid-caria">Caria</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milas">Milas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/becin">Pecin</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> (Balat), founding one of the first maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Menteshe rulers raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands, clashing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, but also traded with Italian merchants in Balat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milas">Milas</a> in the 1330s and praised its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan-bey-of-menteshe">Orhan Bey</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilyas-bey-of-menteshe">Ilyas Bey</a> built the mosque of Balat in 1404 next to the ruins of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a>. Treaties with Venice and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> regulated trade in grain, horses and slaves, and Turkish sailors from Menteshe ports joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aydinids">Aydinids</a> in raids on the islands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> in 1390. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> restored it after defeating Bayezid in 1402, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it back for good in 1424.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/menteshe.jpg" alt="Menteshe" width="320" height="198"><figcaption>Image: Gabagool · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anatolia1300.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1261 CE – 1424 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Milas</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Menteshe Bey, Orhan Bey, Ilyas Bey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Menteşe Beylik, Menteşeoğulları</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1342&amp;focus=menteshe" aria-label="Menteshe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5390 838 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Menteshe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5710 1094L5709 970M5710 1094L6582 1190"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M6582 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5710 1094h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5710 1094h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1342&amp;focus=menteshe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

